column495:
31/Oct/25 5:04 PM;6115198ac51f3a00696f3b0e;h3. ✅ Notes & Best Practices

* You should *log before and after* swapping for traceability.
* If {{115}} or {{128}} is missing, you can skip the swap or handle defaults.
* This logic should only apply to *application messages* (not admin messages like Logon, Heartbeat).
→ You can check:
{noformat}if (message.getHeader().getField(FIX::FIELD::MsgType) != FIX::MsgType_Logon)
    // perform swap
{noformat}
* Ensure that your *session dictionary* allows these fields (include them in FIX dictionary if needed).

----

Would you like me to show how to make this swap logic *bidirectional* — i.e., automatically swap in both directions (for incoming and outgoing) depending on the session role (initiator vs acceptor)? That’s common in gateways and I can show a clean reusable method for that.

h5. You said:

how many tag can be swap

h6. ChatGPT said:

Excellent question — and it’s one that comes up often when building FIX gateways or routers.

Here’s the short and deep answer 👇

----

h3. 🧩 1. Technically: You can swap *any number of FIX tags*

There’s *no hard limit* in the FIX protocol or in QuickFIX on how many tags you can modify, enrich, or swap — as long as:

* Each tag is *defined* in the FIX Data Dictionary (standard or custom).
* You handle them *consistently* (data type and position don’t break message validity).
* You *recalculate BodyLength (9)* and *CheckSum (10)* automatically — QuickFIX does this for you when sending.

So yes, you can swap 2, 5, 10, or 50 tags if needed.

----

h3. ⚙️ 2. Practical implementation pattern

Instead of writing individual swap lines for every pair, you can generalize it.

Example:
Let’s say you want to swap multiple pairs like:

{noformat}115 <-> 128
49  <-> 56
57  <-> 50
{noformat}

You can define this as a *swap map* and iterate dynamically:

h4. ✅ Generic C++ Implementation (QuickFIX)

{noformat}void swapTags(FIX::Message& msg, const std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>& swapPairs)
{
    for (auto& pair : swapPairs)
    {
        int tagA = pair.first;
        int tagB = pair.second;

        std::string valA, valB;
        bool hasA = msg.isSetField(tagA);
        bool hasB = msg.isSetField(tagB);

        if (hasA) msg.getField(valA, tagA);
        if (hasB) msg.getField(valB, tagB);

        // Swap only if at least one exists
        if (hasA || hasB) {
            msg.setField(tagA, valB);
            msg.setField(tagB, valA);
        }
    }
}
{noformat}

Use it like this:

{noformat}std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> swapPairs = {
    {115, 128},  // OnBehalfOfCompID <-> DeliverToCompID
    {49, 56},    // SenderCompID <-> TargetCompID
    {57, 50}     // TargetSubID <-> SenderSubID
};

swapTags(message, swapPairs);
{noformat}

----

h3. 💡 3. When to apply tag swaps

* *Inbound messages* (in {{fromApp}}) → swap for internal consistency.
* *Outbound messages* (in {{toApp}}) → reverse the swap if routing back.
* *Admin messages* → usually _don’t swap_ (e.g. Logon, Heartbeat).

You can check message type before swapping:

{noformat}std::string msgType;
message.getHeader().getField(FIX::FIELD::MsgType, msgType);
if (msgType != FIX::MsgType_Logon && msgType != FIX::MsgType_Heartbeat)
    swapTags(message, swapPairs);
{noformat}

----

h3. 🚀 4. Summary

||Question||Answer||
|How many tags can I swap?|Any number — limited only by your logic, not FIX spec|
|Is it safe?|Yes, as long as message stays valid and BodyLength/Checksum are recalculated|
|Where to do it?|Typically in {{fromApp()}} and {{toApp()}}|
|Best approach?|Use a reusable swap function with a {{std::vector<std::pair<int,int>>}}|

